 JAX allow <strong>252 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>Jaylen Waddle</strong> (WR2) \u2014 JAX leak 44 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Evan Engram</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 JAX are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.31 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><h3>DEN \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>DEN are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (43% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>J.K. Dobbins</strong> (13 carries, 62 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Courtland Sutton</strong> projects for <strong>6 targets and 50 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Evan Engram</strong> (TE, 3 targets, 21 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>JAX \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>JAX are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (43% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Chris Rodriguez Jr.</strong> (11 carries, 51 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Parker Washington</strong> projects for <strong>8 targets and 65 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Brenton Strange</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 37 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touc